<DocsLayout title="Security" description="Security defaults and trust boundaries in Hologram Live.">
  <h1>Security</h1>
  <p>Loopback-only defaults, bounded inputs, explicit authorities, digest verification, typed configuration, and narrow process boundaries form the baseline.</p>

  <h2>Network defaults</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The service binds to loopback by default.</li>
    <li>Non-loopback listeners require bearer-token authentication.</li>
    <li>Non-loopback remote clients require HTTPS.</li>
    <li>gRPC messages and HTTP bodies have independent size limits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fallback safety</h2>
  <p>Authorization denial, authentication failure, TLS validation failure, and integrity failure never fall through to another target. A mutation transport failure is reported as an unknown commit state instead of being retried elsewhere.</p>

  <h2>Content integrity</h2>
  <p>Objects are addressed with BLAKE3. Imported <code>.holo</code> archives are parsed by the pinned archive implementation and their footer fingerprint is verified before catalog admission.</p>

  <h2>Application capabilities</h2>
  <p>A <code>.holo</code> archive contains an untrusted capability request, never its own authority. Live requires canonical upstream <code>CapabilitySet</code> bytes and admits the decoded request against a host-supplied effective grant before provider preparation. The sole archive compatibility exception is the content-address-verified zero-byte sentinel emitted by the early Live compiler, which can only decode to the deny-all empty request; nonempty malformed objects, new source input, and trusted grants remain strict. The default local grant has no storage roots, channels, or network flags. Insufficient authority returns <code>LIVE_AUTHORIZATION_DENIED</code>.</p>
  <p>A child edge's delegated capability object is also untrusted until admitted. Live requires the parent grant to admit that delegation and the delegation to admit the child's requested set. The same rule is applied transitively to nested children. Amplification and under-granted requests return <code>LIVE_AUTHORIZATION_DENIED</code> before the root or any child provider prepares.</p>
  <p><code>--development-grant</code> is restricted to direct local files. Resident development grants come only from trusted service configuration and require a loopback listener; remote callers cannot submit a grant. Capability decision traces contain identities, counts, scalar limits, and allow/deny outcomes, not raw documents or secrets. An awaited JSONL audit row records the principal, relation, application identities, request/delegation κ, grant κ, trusted source, and outcome before provider preparation. It excludes capability documents, roots, channels, tokens, headers, and payload bytes. Successful run and resident results carry only the non-secret request κ, grant κ, trusted-source label, and decision.</p>
  <p>Core-Wasm guest contract v1 has no imports or WASI. The manifest entry selects only a typed export inside the resolved module, never a provider or host function. Source schema v4 writes Wasm <code>contract</code> to canonical <code>aux</code>; exact contract lookup never falls back across identifiers. Component v1 executes through a separate import-free provider with runtime-owned 64 MiB memory, 100 million fuel, 1 MiB input/output, and two-second deadline ceilings. A fresh store isolates each input, while a component-local epoch interrupts timeout, stop, or dropped-future cancellation without affecting another application or core Wasm. Future storage, channel, and mediated network imports require their corresponding admitted fields; clocks, random, environment, process, secrets, inference, and raw sockets remain unavailable while the canonical capability schema cannot represent them. Under-granted imports fail before linker construction.</p>
  <p>Python uses that unchanged import-free Component world. Compilation runs <code>componentize-py 0.25.0 --stub-wasi</code> through an isolated <code>uvx</code> environment with developer Python search paths removed. The compiler chooses an exact PyPI wheel URL/SHA-256 for each supported release host and disables indexes and source builds; unpinned hosts fail closed. External packages must be registry records with exact HTTPS, SHA-256-pinned Python 3 <code>*-none-any.whl</code> artifacts in <code>uv.lock</code>; uv installs only those direct URLs with indexes, dependency re-resolution, source builds, and cache links disabled. Nested source or installed symlinks, native payloads, and native/source-only locks fail closed with rootfs guidance. A versioned compile report records normalized source hashes, the componentizer and dependency artifacts, observed tools, target ABI, and output κ without embedding build-host evidence in canonical application identity. Stubbed randomness repeats a build-time seed inside one component, is not a secure random source, and is reported as a reproducibility blocker; no WASI authority is linked into Live.</p>
  <p>Experimental Python rootfs builds report the requested base and whether it is digest-pinned, hashed source inputs, pinned uv, and—after a real build—observed Docker/image identities and output κ. These observations remain outside the archive and do not turn a mutable tag or Docker export into a reproducible supply-chain claim. Automatic registry digest resolution, normalized OCI construction, SBOMs, and signed attestations remain open.</p>

  <h2>Tracing privacy</h2>
  <p>Tokens, authorization headers, raw prompts, response bodies, private keys, and uploaded bytes must not enter trace fields. OTLP export remains disabled until a collector endpoint is explicitly supplied.</p>

  <h2>Interactive API reference</h2>
  <p>Scalar's JavaScript is served from the Hologram binary. Its optional agent is disabled, and the reference reads the OpenAPI document from the same origin.</p>
